---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/eaton-editor.png';
import compare from '../../assets/img/eaton-compare.png';
import confirm from '../../assets/img/eaton-confirm.png';
import writing from '../../assets/img/eaton-writing.png';
import monitor from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor.png';
import monitorDark from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-dark.png';

// The short case. The deck is one sentence, the ending; the hero is the shipped editor cut to
// its first five rows. Six frames, each a region of one of the Webflow page's own images cut to
// the thing its paragraph names: the first decision carries the comparison view on the words'
// column, the thing the form does that the canvas didn't; the second carries the editor twice
// at the hero's scale, the confirmation inside it and then its header while a write runs; the
// third carries the dark draft dimmed beside the shipped monitor, two across, each with its own
// line to look at. The canvas editor that lost the test isn't shown: only what launched can be,
// and the first decision says so. The snapshot is five one-line rows; the numbers appear once
// each, with what they were measured against, and the close ends on its line.
---
<Case
  title="Eaton — Kyle Xu"
  description="UX Design Intern at Eaton, June–August 2022. RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor for airport engineers. In a comparative usability test the drag-and-drop editor was 35% faster and made 15% more errors, so the form shipped. 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system; in usability testing, operational efficiency +49.7% and error rates −36.8%."
  ogImage="/og-eaton.jpg"
  eyebrow="Eaton · UX Design Intern · June – August 2022"
  deck="The faster of the two editors we tested, 35% quicker, made 15% more errors, so the slower one shipped."
  next={{ title: 'Heygen', to: '/projects/heygen', line: 'The video editor, re-architected around the script.' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">A configuration tool for the drives that run an airport's conveyors.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} name="The editor" evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="RapidLinkX's parameter editor: a blue header and toolbar with a Real-Time switch turned off and a Write In button, linked drives listed on the left with one selected, and a table of five starred parameters with a value field, range, default and display checkbox on each row"
    caption="Real-Time off and Write In in the toolbar; Starred and Compare on the row beneath." />

  <section class="col sec snap" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for the drives in airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor over USB, for airport engineers.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Role</dt>
        <dd>Primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design; my mentor on planning and communication across teams.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Constraints</dt>
        <dd>The hardware was still being designed; regional stakeholders and an external development team each had a say.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Shipped</dt>
        <dd>The editor, the monitor, and 14 component variants added to Eaton's Brightlayer design system.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency up 49.7% and error rates down 36.8%.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-editor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-editor">The slower editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>A wrong value on a baggage line stops the line. The comparative usability test split its participants between a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> canvas and this form; the canvas was quicker and made more errors, and it lost. Under Eaton's NDA I can show only what launched, so the canvas isn't here. Two things gave the form part of its speed back. Starred keeps the table to the parameters that matter on a check. Compare, below, sets the live value beside a saved configuration or the defaults, so a drift reads as a difference rather than a number to hold in your head.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow src={compare} name="Compare" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The comparison view: a breadcrumb reading Parameter Configuration, Parameter Comparison, the title Compare with default values, and a table of four rows with PNU, name, current value, default and range on each"
        caption="The live value beside the default it drifted from, row by row." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-write">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-write">Writing to a drive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>An engineer on a routine round wants the tool linked and watching. One at a drive that has just failed wants nothing written until they've decided what.</p>
      <p>So <span class="nb">real-time</span> writing is a switch, and it's off when the tool opens. Turning it on, or pressing Write In, brings up the same confirmation, with a backup of the drive's current configuration already ticked. While a write runs, the header says so and asks not to be unplugged.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" caption="The backup ticked before anything is written; then the header while the write runs."
        items={[
          { src: confirm, name: 'The confirmation', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The editor with its toolbar, the Real-Time switch off and a Write In button at the right, the linked drives on the left, the starred parameters in the table, and a dialog over the table: Confirm Write-in, a note that changes will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked checkbox to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, and Cancel and Confirm buttons' },
          { src: writing, name: 'The running write', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The editor\'s header and toolbar with a notice laid over their right end: a download icon, Writing to device, Please do not disconnect, and a Cancel button' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-monitor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-monitor">The monitor, in colour</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The same test set a <span class="nb">text-heavy</span> monitor against a <span class="nb">colour-coded</span> chart, and with the chart participants found errors 40% faster. I drew the chart light and dark. The light one shipped because it's the Brightlayer theme the external developers were already building from; a dark monitor would have meant a second theme for one screen. Between the draft and the shipped chart, the four channels moved from axes of their own onto one, where a channel can be read against the others, and their colours were <span class="nb">re-tuned</span> to stay apart once the lines crossed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="pair"
        items={[
          { src: monitorDark, name: 'The dark draft', evidence: 'Concept', dim: true, caption: 'Four channels on four axes of their own.', alt: 'The dark draft of the monitor, everything right of its sidebar: four coloured channel cards in blue, green, purple and orange with live values and four unset, four traces stacked on separate axes over a dark grid with a legend floating over them, and an overview strip beneath with the four traces overlaid' },
          { src: monitor, name: 'The monitor', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'One axis, and the four colours re-tuned to stay apart.', alt: 'The shipped monitor, the same region: Waveform Recording and Error tabs, channels 1 to 4 in blue, green, purple and orange with live values and min and max, channels 5 to 8 reading Please Select, the four traces on one axis over a hundred seconds, and an overview strip beneath' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="close">
    <p class="stance">A wrong value was the expensive thing.</p>
    <p>The form's lost seconds and the confirmation on every write are the price of keeping one out. The chart is there to find the ones that get in.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
